==Episodes==
===Unaired pilot===
The unaired pilot was [[kinescope]]d on March 2, 1951 and was considered lost until the widow of Pepito Pérez, a clown who appears in it, notified [[CBS|CBS Entertainment]] president [[Bud Grant (broadcaster)|Bud Grant]] that she owned a copy given to her husband by Desi Arnaz and Lucille Ball.<ref name="The Washington Post">{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/archive/lifestyle/1990/04/30/love-that-lost-lucy/9cd57b41-0520-4a6d-8d86-0a8a296e4d23/|title=Love That 'Lost' Lucy|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=August 21, 2023}}</ref><ref name="Outsider">{{cite web|url=https://outsider.com/entertainment/on-this-day-long-lost-pilot-i-love-lucy-aied-1990//|title=On This Day: 'Long-Lost' Pilot of ''I Love Lucy'' aired in 1990|work=Outsider.com|access-date=August 21, 2023}}</ref>  The pilot was first broadcast as part of a 60-minute special on CBS television stations nationwide on Monday, April 30, 1990, 39 years after it was originally filmed.<ref name="Outsider" /><ref name="IMDB1951">{{cite web|url=https://www.imdb.com/title/tt0448547/|title=''I Love Lucy: The Very First Show''|work=[[imdb.com]]|access-date=August 22, 2023}}</ref> However, the opening titles and the initial voiceover were damaged or missing, so [[Lucie Arnaz]] read the beginning lines of the opening voiceover, which then segued into the surviving footage.

The original 2003 DVD edition of ''I Love Lucy'' contained the pilot episode with a reconstruction of the opening titles and voiceover. The original 35mm kinescope of the pilot was later located in the CBS archives and was included in the Season 1 [[Blu-ray]] release on August 4, 2015.<ref name="Amazon.com">{{cite web|url=https://m.media-amazon.com/images/S/vse-vms-transcoding-artifact-us-east-1-prod/v2/90747a05-f5fc-5602-8c25-732015ae074b/ShortForm-Generic-480p-16-9-1409173089793-rpcbe5.mp4|title=''I Love Lucy'' Ultimate First Season on Blu-Ray|access-date=August 21, 2023}}</ref>

